{'id': 'arc_task_3c58f95d_rule_horizontal_lines_vertical_tiling_period_lines_solved', 'kind': 'memory', 'title': 'ARC task-3c58f95d rule: horizontal lines -> vertical tiling, period = #lines (SOLVED)', 'content': "ARC task-3c58f95d SOLVED (final: 1 wrong attempt, 2 demos, correct on resubmission). TASK FAMILY 'horizontal/vertical colored lines -> periodic tiling': Input: full-width horizontal colored lines on 0 background (n rows). RULE (verified on 2 demos + test): output row i = color of the input line whose row index ≡ i (mod p), where p = number of colored lines. I.e., collect {row % p: color}, tile that pattern vertically to fill the grid. All n rows get filled because p lines cover all residues mod p. Same rule as diagonal-lines variant (see task_e262a407 and other 3c58f95d memories: diagonal lines extend as periodic stripes). DISTINGUISHING NOTE: this task had only 3 lines -> p=3 pattern [5,3,7] (line rows 0,4,20). Demos had 6 lines -> p=6. KEY LESSON (repeat offender): my 'wrong attempt' was a HAND-TYPED JSON TRANSCRIPTION BUG — submitted a 27-row grid instead of 28 (dropped the last row). ALWAYS generate grids programmatically (json.dump to a file, verify len(grid)==n and all row lengths), and when submitting in a reply, COUNT THE ROWS against the expected dimensions before sending. An INCORRECT verdict may be a malformed grid, not a wrong rule — recheck dimensions/transcription before abandoning a verified rule.", 'path': 'general', 'scope': 'global', 'reference': {

